<x-public-layout title="ATS Explained — What is an ATS?">
    <article class="reference-container ats-guide">
        <header class="ats-guide-intro">
            <span class="reference-eyebrow">ATS EXPLAINED</span>
            <h1>What happens to your CV<br><span>after you click apply?</span></h1>
            <p>An Applicant Tracking System helps employers manage applications. Understanding how it works can help you write a CV that is easier for software to read and recruiters to review.</p>
        </header>

        <section class="ats-guide-section" aria-labelledby="what-is-ats">
            <span class="reference-eyebrow">01 / THE BASICS</span>
            <h2 id="what-is-ats">What is an ATS?</h2>
            <p>ATS stands for <strong>Applicant Tracking System</strong>. It is software employers use to collect and organise job applications, store candidate information and track applicants through the hiring process. Depending on the system and the employer's settings, recruiters can also search, filter or rank applications using job-related criteria.</p>
            <p>Think of it as a recruiter's application workspace. Your CV may be processed there before a person reads it. Different employers use different systems, so there is no single set of rules or universal ATS score.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="ats-guide-section" aria-labelledby="how-ats-works">
            <span class="reference-eyebrow">02 / BEHIND THE APPLICATION</span>
            <h2 id="how-ats-works">How does it read your CV?</h2>
            <div class="ats-guide-grid">
                <div class="ats-guide-card"><span>1. Collect</span><h3>Your application arrives</h3><p>Your uploaded CV and application answers are stored with the role you applied for.</p></div>
                <div class="ats-guide-card"><span>2. Parse</span><h3>Text becomes information</h3><p>A parser tries to identify your name, contact details, job titles, employers, dates, education and skills. Parsing means turning document text into organised fields.</p></div>
                <div class="ats-guide-card"><span>3. Search & review</span><h3>Recruiters find candidates</h3><p>Recruiters may search for skills or use filters and screening questions. Some systems also support matching or ranking. People review applications within the employer's process.</p></div>
            </div>
            <p class="ats-guide-note"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> a CV can look attractive on screen but still have text extracted in the wrong order. Clear structure helps your experience reach the reader accurately.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="ats-guide-section" aria-labelledby="machine-readable">
            <span class="reference-eyebrow">03 / MAKE YOUR EXPERIENCE CLEAR</span>
            <h2 id="machine-readable">What makes a CV easier to read?</h2>
            <ul class="ats-guide-checklist">
                <li><strong>Use a straightforward layout.</strong> A single column is a useful default. Complex tables, text boxes and sidebars can make the reading order less predictable in some parsers.</li>
                <li><strong>Choose familiar headings.</strong> Use labels such as Professional Summary, Work Experience, Education and Skills so each section has an obvious purpose.</li>
                <li><strong>Keep important details as text.</strong> Write out your contact details, skills and qualifications. Avoid relying on icons, photos or skill bars to communicate essential information.</li>
                <li><strong>Make each role easy to identify.</strong> Put the job title, employer and employment dates together, followed by clear bullet points describing your work and achievements.</li>
                <li><strong>Follow the employer's file instructions.</strong> Submit the requested format. For a PDF, make sure the text is selectable rather than a scanned image.</li>
                <li><strong>Check the exported document.</strong> Copy its text into a plain-text editor and read it from top to bottom. This is a useful basic check for missing text or a confused reading order, though it cannot reproduce every ATS.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="ats-guide-section" aria-labelledby="keywords">
            <span class="reference-eyebrow">04 / RELEVANCE MATTERS</span>
            <h2 id="keywords">Use the job description as a guide.</h2>
            <p>Keywords are the terms used to describe the role: skills, tools, qualifications and responsibilities. Recruiters may search for these terms, so use the employer's wording where it accurately describes your experience.</p>
            <div class="ats-guide-example"><h3>A practical example</h3><p>A job asks for <strong>Microsoft Excel</strong> and <strong>inventory management</strong>. If you have that experience, “Used Microsoft Excel to track stock levels and support inventory management” explains more than “Good computer skills”. Add results or figures only when you can support them.</p></div>
            <p>Tailoring means highlighting relevant facts, choosing clear terminology and putting your strongest evidence first. Avoid repeating keywords without context, hiding text or adding skills you do not have.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="ats-guide-section" aria-labelledby="before-after">
            <span class="reference-eyebrow">05 / SAME FACTS, CLEARER PRESENTATION</span>
            <h2 id="before-after">What does an improvement look like?</h2>
            <p>This fictional example shows how clear labels and specific wording can make the same experience easier to understand.</p>
            <div class="ats-guide-grid ats-guide-comparison">
                <div class="ats-guide-card"><span>Before</span><h3>My journey</h3><p>Bright Retail · 2023–2025<br>Helped customers, did stock and computer work.</p><p>Skills: Excel ★★★★☆ · People person</p></div>
                <div class="ats-guide-card"><span>After</span><h3>Work Experience</h3><p><strong>Retail Assistant — Bright Retail</strong><br>2023–2025</p><ul><li>Assisted customers with product enquiries.</li><li>Updated stock records using Microsoft Excel.</li></ul><h3>Skills</h3><p>Customer service · Stock record keeping · Microsoft Excel</p></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="ats-guide-section" aria-labelledby="ats-myths">
            <span class="reference-eyebrow">06 / COMMON QUESTIONS</span>
            <h2 id="ats-myths">What should you keep in mind?</h2>
            <h3>Will an ATS automatically reject my CV?</h3><p>It depends on the employer's process. Some employers configure screening questions or filters, while others use the system mainly to organise applications. Formatting alone does not explain every rejection.</p>
            <h3>Does a high match score guarantee an interview?</h3><p>No. A checker estimates alignment with the information in a job description. It cannot know an employer's private screening criteria, the strength of other applications or the final hiring decision.</p>
            <h3>Should I use PDF or Word?</h3><p>Use the format requested in the job advert or application portal. Check that your final file contains readable text and that its layout survives export.</p>
            <h3>Can AI improve my CV?</h3><p>AI can help clarify wording and highlight relevant experience. Review every suggestion, keep your dates and qualifications accurate, and remove anything you cannot substantiate.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="ats-guide-section" aria-labelledby="ats-why-it-matters">
            <span class="reference-eyebrow">07 / WHY IT MATTERS</span>
            <h2 id="ats-why-it-matters">Give your experience a clear introduction.</h2>
            <p>Your CV is your opportunity to explain what you can contribute. Make it easy to find your relevant skills, understand your work history and see the evidence behind your achievements.</p>
            <p>Before you apply, check three things: can the document's text be selected and read in a sensible order, are your roles and qualifications clearly labelled, and have you explained how your actual experience relates to the job?</p>
            <p>You cannot control an employer's screening criteria or hiring decision. You can control the accuracy, relevance and readability of the CV you submit. Focus on presenting your experience clearly, rather than chasing a perfect match score.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="ats-guide-cta" aria-labelledby="ats-next-step">
            <span class="reference-eyebrow">YOUR NEXT STEP</span>
            <h2 id="ats-next-step">Build a CV that tells your story clearly.</h2>
            <p>Use atscvdetector to build your document or compare your existing CV with a job description. Keep your experience accurate, review your changes and check the final file before you apply.</p>
